{"author":{"id":"2f242a7e7f2592f8909954a77fabece74f6e8cc197182647ed3b15ea8e3878af"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791133074,"nonce":"KxUmwo1fUlaBLFc3PKwcOvkNoKQ=","title":"a transparent window cannot drop the system shadow or choose its blur","message":"ashui (2026-10-04) draws clear glass in a transparent borderless window. On macOS the shadow of a non-opaque window is traced from its alpha, outlining every alpha edge and glyph, and winit's set_blur always blurs the background with radius 80, a heavy frost.\n\nDone in 8d581e727c4560717acc114867540a77959c9ebc: WindowAttributes.hasShadow and blurRadius, Window.setHasShadow and setBlurRadius. On macOS, has_shadow, and the radius through the private CGSSetWindowBackgroundBlurRadius call winit itself makes, on the window number found from the raw handle through libobjc. On Windows the shadow is the undecorated shadow. Elsewhere the radius is blur on or off, and the shadow does nothing.\n\nChecked on macOS:\n- With the shadow off, screencapture -l of the window is no bigger than the frame (600x464); turned on at runtime it grows to 824x688; turned off again it returns to the frame.\n- The window number found through libobjc matched CGWindowList's id.\n- In one run, the region behind the transparent window lost most of its detail when the radius went from 0 to 10 (mean neighbour difference 3.01 to 0.29).\n\nhlwindow 1fac0140d6e6133542fc0be92c59156f7434df93 moves to it.","files":null}]}